{
  "requirement": "Model tool descriptions SHOULD NOT explain cases the model meets only\noccasionally, such as access policy, excluded or build-output paths, redaction,\npaging, and failures, and the harness SHOULD explain each of these where it\noccurs: in the tool error, or in a note in the result it affects. The model reads\nevery description on every assessment, so explaining rare cases there costs\nattention on every review for a case most never meet, and access-policy wording\nin particular can steer its reasoning away from the review. A tool whose purpose\nis one of these cases, such as `next_page`, explains it in its description.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The reviewed tests cover both halves of `requirements.authoring.tool_descriptions.special_cases`: descriptions stay free of rare-case wording, and the harness explains each rare case where it occurs.\n\n**Descriptions SHOULD NOT explain rare cases**\n- `src/authoring/tools.rs#mod[tests]/fn[descriptions_leave_paging_policy_redaction_and_failures_to_results]` loops over every tool definition. It asserts that no lowercased description contains any of these terms: \"kib\", \"returned in pages\", \"redact\", \"credential\", \"denied\", \"disallow\", \"habeas.toml\", \"aborts\", \"untracked\", \"build output\", \"outside the project\", \"tool error\", \"policy\", \"ignore rules\", \"exclusion\", \"permitted\".\n- The same test asserts `next_page` has the exact expected parameter schema.\n- The `next_page` description is deliberately paging-focused, as the requirement allows. It is checked for the paging-limit and policy wording above, not for the word \"page\".\n- `fn[descriptions_give_each_argument_and_the_result_form]` pins the `next_page` description's content as the tool whose purpose is paging.\n- The term list is a heuristic and could miss a paraphrase such as \"only tracked files are readable\". I don't treat that as a gap. The list covers the categories the requirement names, and a text-based check cannot be exhaustive.\n\n**The harness explains each case where it occurs**\n- **Access policy** (untracked files, disallow rules, outside the project, symlink escape):\n  - `fn[untracked_diagnostics_name_git_add_and_an_allow_rule]` asserts the tool error names `git add`, the `habeas.toml allow rule` and the path.\n  - `fn[unpermitted_requests_outside_the_project_are_recoverable_tool_errors]` asserts the error says \"outside the project\" for every tool.\n  - `fn[a_read_through_a_symlink_leaving_the_project_returns_an_error_to_the_model]` asserts the error reaches the model naming the path and the reason.\n  - `fn[evidence_rules_are_literal_paths_not_globs]` asserts the \"disallowed by habeas.toml rule\" message.\n- **Excluded or build-output paths:**\n  - `fn[generated_evidence_is_excluded_from_reads_search_and_discovery]` asserts the error mentions \"generated evidence\" and the path across habeas, list_files, ripgrep and ast_grep, including through symlinks.\n  - `fn[requests_for_book_or_target_output_are_ordinary_read_failures]` asserts the \"holds build output … the sources it is built from are elsewhere in the project\" text for every tool. It covers both the default `book` directory and a custom build-dir, and covers `target`.\n- **Redaction:** `fn[only_results_with_redacted_text_carry_the_redaction_note]` asserts the `note` field equals `REDACTION_NOTE` on redacted habeas and ripgrep results and on requirement and definition results. It asserts the note is absent on unredacted results, including source that literally contains the marker.\n- **Paging:**\n  - `src/authoring/pages.rs#mod[tests]/fn[large_listings_and_outlines_page_whole_entries_in_order]` calls the helper `all_pages`. That helper asserts every page with more to come carries `page.note == PAGE_NOTE`, and that the last page has no note.\n  - The page-note behaviour and the separate redaction note are also asserted in `fn[redacted_results_reserve_space_for_the_note_on_every_delivered_page]`.\n- **Failures:** `src/authoring/mod.rs#mod[tests]/fn[malformed_and_failed_reads_return_to_the_model_and_the_assessment_continues]` covers malformed arguments, a missing file, an absent item and an out-of-range line. It asserts each failure message appears in the second request to the model and not in the first. It also asserts the failed file is not recorded as evidence.\n\nEvery named category (access policy, excluded and build-output paths, redaction, paging, failures) has a test that triggers the case and asserts the explanation in the tool error or result note. The tests are marked with `{test}` references to the subject. I found no unmarked relevant tests and no acknowledged gaps.",
  "references": "09fa97af32a9a041637594fa3e677cc1cd11fbb6d4e5831cec4d6e2035832ab5",
